						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=154.msg1972#msg1972"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=154.msg1972#msg1972">Southern Rap Appreciation Thread</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> October 21, 2006, 12:17:45 am &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">Whats up everyone... as I said I am new to the board, and was linked by a friend who a few months ago introduced me to Common Market.&nbsp; I enjoy the music a lot, but I have eclectic taste in music, especially rap music.&nbsp; It seems like the majority of the patrons of this board lean towards the more &quot;conscious&quot; rap styles, and more often than not that excludes southern rap music.&nbsp; They seem to certainly catch a lot of flack from purists, and in my opinion a lot of that isn&#039;t warranted.&nbsp; I am going to list some of my favorite southern rap albums, and if youre a fan, feel free to do so as well.&nbsp; A healthy, spirited dissertation concerning the role of the south in hip hop is encouraged as well.&nbsp; Lets get down to the list.<br /><br /><img src="http://ec1.images-amazon.com/images/P/B00000JCOT.01._SS500_SCMZZZZZZZ_V1057212474_.jpg" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />Project Pat - Ghetty Green<br /><br />This one is excellent.&nbsp; Project is as much of a lyricist as Stephen Hawking is an acrobat, but when he does rap, it&#039;s funny.&nbsp; Whether or not it&#039;s intentional is irrelevant imo, but as a piece of art it stands based solely upon the fact he is passionate about his topics.&nbsp; In case you were sleeping on the DJ Paul and Juicy J beats, this album is beyond ill.&nbsp; Their sample game is on point and they are nice on the keys too.<br /><br />Recommended songs: 528-Cash, Slangin&#039; Rocks, Up there, Represent it, Choices, Ballers<br /><br /><img src="http://ec3.images-amazon.com/images/P/B00000DFVI.01._SS500_SCLZZZZZZZ_V1115936342_.jpg" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />Tear Da Club Up Thugs - Crazyndalastdayz<br /><br />Another HCP gem.&nbsp; This album yet again has... interesting lyrics and very good production.&nbsp; For those of you who are not familiar with tear da club up, its 3-6 mafia and the artist on their label, Hypnotize Camp Posse.&nbsp; It&#039;s not the most progressive album, but this is back when they would talk about satan every so often.&nbsp; If you ask me, it&#039;s a gem.&nbsp; <br /><br />Recommended Songs: Who&#039;s the crunkest, Push Em Off, Big Business, Triple Six Clubhouse, Get Buck, Get Wild<br /><br /><img src="http://ec1.images-amazon.com/images/P/B00006C2H3.01._SS500_SCLZZZZZZZ_V1116245545_.jpg" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />Scarface - The Fix<br /><br />This one is crazy.&nbsp; Whole album is dope, go cop it.<br /><br />Recommended Songs: All of them, but check the Nas verse on In Between Us<br /><br /><img src="http://ec1.images-amazon.com/images/P/B00000053Q.01._SS500_SCLZZZZZZZ_V1115291125_.jpg" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />UGK - Ridin Dirty<br /><br />Their albums have a real ill, blues feel to them.&nbsp; Bun B is nice but Pimp C is good at talking about trashy shit.&nbsp; This makes for a nice balance.&nbsp; <br /><br />Recommended Songs: Murder, Good Stuff, Hi-Life, Touched<br /><br /><img src="http://ec3.images-amazon.com/images/P/B00000JCIA.01._SS500_SCMZZZZZZZ_V1056649143_.jpg" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />Fiend - Street Life<br /><br />This album is dark, dark, dark.&nbsp; I can listen to this one all day long.&nbsp; Check it out, you will see.<br /><br />Recommended songs: Mr Womp Womp, The Rock Show, Trip to London, War 4 Reason<br /><br /><img src="http://ec1.images-amazon.com/images/P/B00000I7K7.01._SS500_SCLZZZZZZZ_V1116197313_.jpg" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />C Murder - Bossaline<br /><br />Good beats, bad raps.&nbsp; It&#039;s your typical no limit CD but it is done well, with mr Gnarls Barkley himself Cee-Lo showing up on a song when he used to be down with Goodie Mob.&nbsp; (didnt he rejoin the group?)<br /><br />Recommended Songs: Ghetto Boy, Ride On Dem Bustas, Still Makin Moves, Murder and Daz, Where We Wanna, Like A Jungle<br /><br />Check some of these out if you aren&#039;t familiar with them, and who knows, you might like some of it.<br /><br />Matt</div>
						</td>
